Hi! My name is Charlie and I am a backend developer based in Silicon Valley.

I have been working on a new C# application at work and I am starting to regularize it using LINQ to maintain data integrity at runtime. LINQ stands for Language Integrated Query, and it works with entities and queries in an object model.

My code is currently hard-coded to connect to a specific database and retrieve records. As the database model changes, I will need to update my code to keep it synchronized.

However, I want to be able to write my LINQ queries in a way that is agnostic of the specific database type or connection string. Ideally, I could specify my database connection details using environment variables, instead of hard-coding them in my code.

Here's a code snippet of my to do list application, using C# and SQLite:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Linq;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

public class TodoManager : DbContext
{
    private static readonly string dbName = "todos.db";

    public DbSet<TodoItem> Items { get; set; }

    protected override void OnConfiguring(DbContextOptionsBuilder optionsBuilder)
    {
        optionsBuilder.UseSqlite($"Filename={dbName}");
    }

    public void PrintAllItems()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Items.Where(t => t.Status == Status.Complete));
    }
}
```

As you can see, the `DbContext` is hard-coded to use SQLite as the database. How can I refactor my code to allow me to use a different database?
